  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Saturday)\nIssued at 126 PM CDT SUN JUL 5 2026\n\nShowers and storms are slowly working their way northeast this\nmorning, as the main MCV we'll be watching this afternoon moves\ninto the Mississippi Delta region. This remnant MCV will be the\ncatalyst for more organized thunderstorm activity this afternoon,\nwith some of this activity expected to linger into the overnight\nhours. Given plenty of instability, a few strong to severe storms\nwill be possible today, with damaging winds being the main\nthreat. Thankfully, these severe probabilities are low, and\nwidespread severe weather is not expected.\n\nBy tomorrow, a weak trough will have worked into the region via\nthe Midwest/Great Lakes, allowing for more organized thunderstorm\nactivity to remain in the forecast. With that being said, the\nbest forcing will likely be along and north of I-20, so this is\nwhere the best rain coverage is anticipated. This trough will\nbecome partially disconnected from the main flow aloft during\nthis time, forming a pseudo \"closed low\" that will more or less\nmeander across the Tennessee River Valley into Thursday. Rain\nprobabilities will be on the lower end of the spectrum come\nWednesday and Thursday, as we'll be well south of the best\nforcing, but that won't exclude us from scattered, diurnally\ndriven thunderstorms into the weekend.\n\nAs we head into the weekend, it appears like another weak trough\ncould sneak its way into the area, as another system works\nthrough the Midwest once again. The overall confidence with this\nweekend trough currently remains low, with the main implications\nhere being organized rain chances, or more scattered rain chances\nif the trough doesn't materialize. Otherwise, afternoon high\ntemperatures will range in the upper-80s and low-90s given the\nincreased cloud cover, so no additional heat headlines are\nexpected at this time.\n\n/44/",
